WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:11.710
<v Speaker 1>Welcome to our deep dive for you today into Reza Shakazemi's spiritual ethos, exploring the letters anatomy of a whole society and the sharpest test of any just rule, the claim of the poor.

2
00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:17.150
<v Speaker 1>This is onerous for the governors, but God makes it light for those who aspire to the hereafter.

3
00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:21.550
<v Speaker 1>Today we're looking at a document that is, well, it's over 1000 years old,

4
00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:26.670
<v Speaker 1>but it somehow diagnoses the exact dysfunctions of modern political and corporate leadership.

5
00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:27.510
<v Speaker 2>It really does.

6
00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.630
<v Speaker 2>It's almost unsettling how timely it is.

7
00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:30.910
<v Speaker 1>I know, it's striking.

8
00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:38.550
<v Speaker 1>And our mission today is to figure out how a leader can hold immense structural power without that power completely corrupting their soul.

9
00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:45.230
<v Speaker 1>We're going to explore how a ruler's justice flows from his hidden tie to the real, not from any sort of worldly calculation.

10
00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:47.070
<v Speaker 2>Which is such a pivot from where we were.

11
00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:50.670
<v Speaker 2>Up until this point in the text, we've been looking at the inward construction of a leader.

12
00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:51.750
<v Speaker 2>We've examined the

13
00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:57.750
<v Speaker 2>the mastery of anger, the mercy owed to subjects, but now the inward man has turned outward.

14
00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.710
<v Speaker 1>He suddenly has to govern an entire society.

15
00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:05.390
<v Speaker 2>Oh, a massive society made of competing parts, competing classes, and totally competing needs.

16
00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:11.870
<v Speaker 2>And this transition, this move from the internal to the external is incredibly fraught with danger.

17
00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:16.750
<v Speaker 2>It's where the true test lies for any person in a position of authority.

18
00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:17.230
<v Speaker 2>Because.

19
00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:22.430
<v Speaker 1>It's one thing to describe the ideal ruler's soul in a vacuum, isn't it?

20
00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:24.670
<v Speaker 1>Just sitting quietly in contemplation.

21
00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:25.150
<v Speaker 2>Sure.

22
00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:34.270
<v Speaker 2>It is an entirely different undertaking to describe the actual grinding machine of state that this ruler must operate, all while maintaining that inner peace.

23
00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:39.230
<v Speaker 2>The letter we are examining is basically attempting to keep these two descriptions from coming apart.

24
00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:40.510
<v Speaker 1>It's a massive wager.

25
00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:44.430
<v Speaker 1>The wager here is that the same conviction which orders a person's inner life

26
00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:47.310
<v Speaker 1>can actually successfully order a whole kingdom.

27
00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:56.710
<v Speaker 2>Provided that the leader never forgets on whose behalf they hold the office, they must never ever mistake the center they occupy for a strength that originates within themselves.

28
00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:04.670
<v Speaker 2>And if you think about your own life out there, whether you manage a household or a small team at work or an entire organization, you've probably felt this tension.

29
00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:05.350
<v Speaker 1>Oh, definitely.

30
00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:12.110
<v Speaker 2>You're placed in a position where everyone looks to you for direction, yet internally you're just acutely aware of your own limitations.

31
00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:15.070
<v Speaker 1>That brings us to the fundamental crisis of the unseen axle.

32
00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:19.870
<v Speaker 1>This is the first governing analogy we find in the text, and it's so vital for you to grasp.

33
00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:25.310
<v Speaker 1>The letter leaves the governor he counsels in what looks like a completely impossible position.

34
00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:26.270
<v Speaker 2>Totally impossible.

35
00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:28.750
<v Speaker 1>He is praised at the exact center of society.

36
00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:31.710
<v Speaker 1>Every single thread of the state runs back to him.

37
00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:35.070
<v Speaker 1>You've got the soldiers, the scribes, the judges, the tax collectors.

38
00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:36.990
<v Speaker 1>They all just revolve around him.

39
00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:41.230
<v Speaker 1>Yet for all that centrality, he is utterly powerless in himself.

40
00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:43.150
<v Speaker 1>Society is a wheel.

41
00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:46.350
<v Speaker 1>The governor is the hub and the classes are the spokes.

42
00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:50.750
<v Speaker 1>But a hub turns only on an unseen axle, joining it to the real.

43
00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:54.670
<v Speaker 2>What is so compelling about this image is how it fundamentally redefines leadership.

44
00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:58.350
<v Speaker 2>It shifts the focus from pushing the wheel to depending on the axle.

45
00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.830
<v Speaker 2>I mean, think about the physical nature of a wheel for a second.

46
00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:02.350
<v Speaker 1>It's a great mechanical image.

47
00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:03.070
<v Speaker 2>It is.

48
00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:07.390
<v Speaker 2>A hub is the most important part of the wheel, but it's also the most helpless.

49
00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:08.990
<v Speaker 2>Everything depends on it.

50
00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:14.270
<v Speaker 2>But it depends, in turn, on something it cannot supply from within its own circular boundary.

51
00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:18.790
<v Speaker 2>The hub is just dead weight without the vertical axis connecting it to the absolute.

52
00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:20.510
<v Speaker 1>Dead weight, that's the perfect phrase.

53
00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:26.110
<v Speaker 2>Because we constantly view political or social leadership as a horizontal exertion of force.

54
00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:32.350
<v Speaker 2>We think of administration, policy, decree, manipulation, strategy, all those horizontal actions.

55
00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:39.150
<v Speaker 1>We assume a strong leader is someone who grips the spokes and just forces the wheel forward through sheer willpower.

56
00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:44.030
<v Speaker 2>But all of those horizontal efforts mean absolutely nothing if the vertical line to the reel is severed.

57
00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:50.510
<v Speaker 2>The hub sits at the center of every human relationship in the state, but if that vertical connection is cut, it turns nothing at all.

58
00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:52.830
<v Speaker 2>The classes are the visible wheel.

59
00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:54.510
<v Speaker 2>The axle is invisible.

60
00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:57.150
<v Speaker 2>And only the axle makes the wheel a wheel.

61
00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:00.350
<v Speaker 1>Let's translate that into something deeply practical for the listener.

62
00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:06.270
<v Speaker 1>Imagine a modern chief executive officer trying to steer a massive multinational corporation.

63
00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:09.230
<v Speaker 1>They can pull all the horizontal levers available to them, right?

64
00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:13.390
<v Speaker 2>Sure, they can hire aggressive managers, overhaul corporate policies.

65
00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:14.550
<v Speaker 1>Launch new products.

66
00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:16.510
<v Speaker 1>restructure the entire hierarchy.

67
00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:29.470
<v Speaker 1>But if their unseen axle, their core ethical operating principle, their connection to a truth higher than next quarter's profit margins is broken or dismissing entirely, the whole corporate wheel eventually spins out of control.

68
00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:31.150
<v Speaker 2>It becomes incredibly erratic.

69
00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:36.990
<v Speaker 1>The spokes, meaning the employees, the customers, the stakeholders, they begin to snap under the friction.

70
00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:40.910
<v Speaker 1>The CEO might occupy the center of the organization chart,

71
00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:46.510
<v Speaker 1>But without that vertical grounding, their horizontal power only accelerates the collapse.

72
00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:51.550
<v Speaker 2>And that forces us to ask a very difficult question about the nature of authority itself.

73
00:04:51.950 --> 00:05:02.110
<v Speaker 2>If A leader's power does not come from within themselves, and if their horizontal efforts are useless without a vertical anchor, we simply have to redefine what power actually is.

74
00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:05.430
<v Speaker 1>The text does not allow us to view power as a personal possession.

75
00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:09.350
<v Speaker 1>It's not a prize to be won at the end of a grueling political campaign.

76
00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:09.710
<v Speaker 2>Not at all.

77
00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:10.670
<v Speaker 1>The Imam,

78
00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:14.830
<v Speaker 1>peace be upon him, views authorities strictly as a trial from God.

79
00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:18.110
<v Speaker 1>It is not a privilege elevated over the subjects.

80
00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:22.830
<v Speaker 1>That single perspective carries the entirety of his attitude toward rulership.

81
00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:26.190
<v Speaker 1>He accepted power only as an unavoidable duty.

82
00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:29.070
<v Speaker 1>It was an agonizing burden placed upon his shoulders.

83
00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:37.710
<v Speaker 2>The imagery used to describe this reluctant acceptance is intensely vivid, and it's intentionally undignified.

84
00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:50.510
<v Speaker 2>In a famous sermon, he declares that if God had not taken a promise from the learned that they would not stand by silently in the face of the rapacity of the tyrant nor the hunger of the oppressed, he would have flung the reins of the caliphate back upon its widowers.

85
00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:52.030
<v Speaker 1>He goes even further than that, actually.

86
00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:57.790
<v Speaker 1>He states that this world of political power is as insignificant to him as that which drifts from the nose of a goat.

87
00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:00.310
<v Speaker 2>We really need to pause on that image, the sneeze of a goat.

88
00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:02.910
<v Speaker 2>It is such a jarring, repulsive metaphor.

89
00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:03.630
<v Speaker 1>It's meant to be.

90
00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:07.950
<v Speaker 2>Most leaders in human history, they commission grand portraits of themselves.

91
00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:09.150
<v Speaker 2>statues.

92
00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:11.950
<v Speaker 2>They speak of their office with this towering reverence.

93
00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:16.270
<v Speaker 2>But here, the highest office in the land is compared to animal mucus.

94
00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:23.310
<v Speaker 2>The reins belong to the caliphate, but the withers are the back of the beast he would gladly let run off into the desert.

95
00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:26.990
<v Speaker 1>The only thing keeping him in the saddle is not an appetite for the office.

96
00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:28.350
<v Speaker 1>But A covenant?

97
00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:35.150
<v Speaker 2>A covenant that forbids the knowing person to remain a passive bystander while the strong devour the weak.

98
00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:38.830
<v Speaker 2>Power in this framework, it weighs like a crushing debt.

99
00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:44.310
<v Speaker 1>Let me push back on this framework for a moment and make a strong case for political ambition just to test the strength of this idea.

100
00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:46.390
<v Speaker 1>I mean, doesn't ambition drive progress?

101
00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:47.630
<v Speaker 2>That's the usual argument.

102
00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:53.070
<v Speaker 1>Is it not the very thing that compels capable people to take on difficult, thankless, and exhausting offices?

103
00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:57.310
<v Speaker 1>We structure our entire modern democratic apparatus on the idea that

104
00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:00.150
<v Speaker 1>Individuals must fiercely desire the office in order to win it.

105
00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:01.470
<v Speaker 2>They spend years doing it.

106
00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:06.910
<v Speaker 1>They campaign for years, they raise millions of dollars, they subject their families to intense public scrutiny.

107
00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:13.470
<v Speaker 1>The assumption is that this very desire for power is what fuels their endurance once they are finally sitting behind the desk.

108
00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:17.870
<v Speaker 1>If a leader truly viewed the office with the disgust of a goat sneeze,

109
00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:22.270
<v Speaker 1>Wouldn't they just be completely ineffective, like apathetic?

110
00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:24.830
<v Speaker 2>That is the conventional logic of ambition.

111
00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:30.110
<v Speaker 2>It's the air we breathe in modern political life, but the text overturns it completely.

112
00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:33.910
<v Speaker 2>And we find a striking parallel in Plato, actually, from the Republic.

113
00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:35.310
<v Speaker 1>Oh, that's a brilliant connection.

114
00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:40.910
<v Speaker 2>For Plato, the true philosopher who consents to rule is precisely the one who does not want to rule.

115
00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:48.670
<v Speaker 2>The ideal leader takes office not regarding it as a distinction, not as a crown to be worn, but as an unavoidable task.

116
00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:53.630
<v Speaker 2>They care only for justice as the one indispensable thing the state exists to serve.

117
00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:58.270
<v Speaker 1>Both Plato and the text we're examining reverse the ordinary logic of human ambition.

118
00:07:58.430 --> 00:08:02.270
<v Speaker 2>The office is not a reward the ruler collects for being clever or popular.

119
00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:06.990
<v Speaker 2>It is a massive burden the community lays on the shoulders best able to bear it.

120
00:08:07.430 --> 00:08:11.230
<v Speaker 2>And the best shoulders are the ones that feel its full, terrifying weight

121
00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:13.230
<v Speaker 2>glamorous prestige.

122
00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:19.870
<v Speaker 1>Which brings us beautifully to the second governing analogy, drawn from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family.

123
00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:24.030
<v Speaker 1>The ruler is a shepherd who owns nothing of the flock yet answers for it.

124
00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:26.510
<v Speaker 2>That's such a grounding image.

125
00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:33.310
<v Speaker 1>The text states that the blessings of God upon a person do not become immense without the right of God over him becoming equally immense.

126
00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:35.070
<v Speaker 1>Privilege obligates.

127
00:08:35.790 --> 00:08:38.750
<v Speaker 1>This is a profound shift in perspective for anyone listening.

128
00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:46.230
<v Speaker 1>To be granted knowledge or wealth or structural power is not to be handed a treasure chest to spend on your own desires.

129
00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:47.550
<v Speaker 2>It's a claim against yourself.

130
00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:49.710
<v Speaker 1>The more one is received, the more is asked.

131
00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:53.870
<v Speaker 1>A governor is simply an individual to whom a very great deal has been given.

132
00:08:54.390 --> 00:08:57.390
<v Speaker 1>and therefore an individual against whom God's claim runs very deep.

133
00:08:57.710 --> 00:09:01.630
<v Speaker 2>Holding a class of people in trust is to be handed sheep that were never your own.

134
00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:05.470
<v Speaker 2>You do not own the wool, you do not own the milk, you do not own the pasture.

135
00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:11.150
<v Speaker 2>You merely watch over them, knowing that at the end of the day, the true owner will ask you how you kept them.

136
00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:12.750
<v Speaker 1>Were they protected from the wolves?

137
00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:14.830
<v Speaker 1>Did you lead them to safe water?

138
00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:21.630
<v Speaker 2>And this immense burden of answerability dictates every single decision a leader makes, starting with the company they keep.

139
00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:28.190
<v Speaker 2>The letter issues a very specific, uncompromising warning to the governor regarding his inner circle.

140
00:09:28.190 --> 00:09:32.030
<v Speaker 1>He's told to explicitly exclude three types of people from his council.

141
00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:41.070
<v Speaker 2>The miser who will frighten him from generosity, the coward who will make him doubt his own strength, and the greedy person who will dress up avarice as good sense.

142
00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:50.750
<v Speaker 1>The text summarizes this exclusion by stating that miserliness, cowardice, and avarice are diverse inclinations, but they are all united by a single underlying disease.

143
00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:53.230
<v Speaker 1>And that disease is a bad opinion of God.

144
00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:57.790
<v Speaker 2>That sounds like wordplay, how is a bad opinion of God different from plain selfishness?

145
00:09:58.190 --> 00:10:08.830
<v Speaker 2>It seems like we are taking ordinary human flaws, things we see every day in boardrooms and government halls, and elevating them to a grand theological status just to make them sound more severe.

146
00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:10.190
<v Speaker 1>It's a sharp challenge.

147
00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:15.630
<v Speaker 1>But the text makes a profound claim here that we must earn and truly understand.

148
00:10:16.030 --> 00:10:19.710
<v Speaker 1>The term used to describe this condition is su'alzan.

149
00:10:20.150 --> 00:10:21.950
<v Speaker 1>It is a bad opinion of God.

150
00:10:21.950 --> 00:10:23.550
<v Speaker 2>So it's not just a passing feeling.

151
00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:25.310
<v Speaker 1>It is not merely a mood.

152
00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:27.390
<v Speaker 1>It's not a momentary lapse in judgment.

153
00:10:27.550 --> 00:10:29.550
<v Speaker 1>It is a mistaken theology.

154
00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:37.070
<v Speaker 1>It is a catastrophic misreading of the divine nature that quietly produces these vices as its inevitable symptoms.

155
00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:39.950
<v Speaker 1>To put it simply, a small God makes small men.

156
00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:41.630
<v Speaker 2>Let us trace the psychology of that.

157
00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:43.550
<v Speaker 2>Consider the inner life of the miser.

158
00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:45.950
<v Speaker 2>The miser is not just someone who likes keeping their money.

159
00:10:46.190 --> 00:10:50.070
<v Speaker 2>Deep down, the miser secretly believes the universe is a place of absolute

160
00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:50.830
<v Speaker 2>scarcity.

161
00:10:51.070 --> 00:10:56.190
<v Speaker 2>They believe God may not provide for them tomorrow, therefore they must violently hoard what they have today.

162
00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:57.470
<v Speaker 1>They're terrified of running out.

163
00:10:57.630 --> 00:11:00.110
<v Speaker 2>The coward operates on a similar frequency.

164
00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:08.270
<v Speaker 2>The coward secretly believes God may not protect them when they stand up for what is right, therefore they must shrink from duty and hide behind compromises.

165
00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:19.470
<v Speaker 1>And the greedy person secretly believes God has given them less than they truly need to be happy, therefore they must constantly, restlessly grasp for more, stepping on anyone in their path to get it.

166
00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:25.230
<v Speaker 1>Trace each of these vices to its root and you find the exact same false idea of who God is.

167
00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:27.230
<v Speaker 1>The fault is not first in the will.

168
00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:28.830
<v Speaker 1>It is an intellectual dysfunction.

169
00:11:29.070 --> 00:11:31.230
<v Speaker 1>It's a fault in the spiritual understanding.

170
00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:34.830
<v Speaker 1>It is the bitter fruit of a weak, emaciated faith.

171
00:11:35.390 --> 00:11:40.510
<v Speaker 1>To deny God's reality entirely is just the most extreme form of holding a bad opinion of him.

172
00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:48.830
<v Speaker 2>And when a leader surrounds themselves with advisors who operate from this mindset of scarcity and fear, those advisors will inevitably shape the leader's policies.

173
00:11:49.070 --> 00:11:55.390
<v Speaker 1>A miserly advisor will convince a governor to cut funding for the vulnerable, framing it as fiscal responsibility.

174
00:11:55.630 --> 00:12:01.310
<v Speaker 2>A cowardly advisor will convince a governor to ignore a glaring injustice, framing it as political pragmatism.

175
00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:05.230
<v Speaker 2>The advisor's bad theology infects the entire state apparatus.

176
00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:09.710
<v Speaker 1>If we follow that logic to its natural conclusion, the converse must also be true.

177
00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:16.910
<v Speaker 1>A strong, living, expansive faith generates the opposite condition, which is Husn al-Zan.

178
00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:23.070
<v Speaker 1>This is a truer, deeper perception of the divine reality as infinitely abundant and merciful.

179
00:12:23.470 --> 00:12:28.110
<v Speaker 2>Out of that perception, the corresponding virtues grow organically of their own accord.

180
00:12:28.510 --> 00:12:31.710
<v Speaker 2>Character is entirely downstream of belief about God.

181
00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:32.750
<v Speaker 1>Think of it like a river.

182
00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:44.110
<v Speaker 2>If the water at the source is polluted by fear and a belief in scarcity, everything downstream, your actions, your policies, your relationships, they will all be polluted by selfishness and cowardice.

183
00:12:44.510 --> 00:12:47.710
<v Speaker 2>But if the source is pure, the downstream waters bring life.

184
00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:52.750
<v Speaker 1>The person who knows God is fundamentally generous can afford to be generous themselves.

185
00:12:53.270 --> 00:12:58.430
<v Speaker 1>The person who trusts God's ultimate protection can afford the staggering courage required to face down tyranny.

186
00:12:58.430 --> 00:13:01.590
<v Speaker 2>The person certain of God's provision has zero need to hoard wealth.

187
00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:07.790
<v Speaker 2>Advisors who counsel meanness and fear are theologians of a small God, and they will make the ruler's God small too.

188
00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:12.110
<v Speaker 1>The method to acquire these virtues is not just trying harder to be good.

189
00:13:12.590 --> 00:13:17.070
<v Speaker 1>It is to contemplate the divine qualities and let the soul be reshaped by them.

190
00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:20.510
<v Speaker 1>You strive to be generous because God is the generous.

191
00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:24.830
<v Speaker 1>You strive to be merciful and compassionate because He is the merciful and the compassionate.

192
00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:30.270
<v Speaker 2>You never do these things because you imagine they originate in your own brilliance or inherent goodness.

193
00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:34.910
<v Speaker 2>Emulating God's generosity requires A profound self-effacement.

194
00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:39.390
<v Speaker 2>You must balance servanthood with the duty of representing God.

195
00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:41.550
<v Speaker 1>That balance is incredibly delicate.

196
00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:46.990
<v Speaker 1>Servanthood keeps the representative from sliding into the trap of mistaking themselves for what they represent.

197
00:13:47.390 --> 00:13:51.790
<v Speaker 1>It prevents the arrogance of thinking, I am the source of justice in this land.

198
00:13:51.870 --> 00:13:58.990
<v Speaker 2>At the same time, the duty of representing God keeps the servant from collapsing into a paralyzed forgetfulness of the dignity their station carries.

199
00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:01.630
<v Speaker 2>It prevents them from saying, I am just a lowly servant.

200
00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:05.150
<v Speaker 2>I cannot possibly fix these systemic issues, so I will do nothing.

201
00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:09.230
<v Speaker 1>It is a dynamic tension between humility and profound responsibility.

202
00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:11.830
<v Speaker 2>That balance is a form of justice itself.

203
00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:15.870
<v Speaker 2>It's the placing of each thing in its rightful order within the soul.

204
00:14:16.070 --> 00:14:20.830
<v Speaker 1>And this inner disposition is the absolute prerequisite for everything that follows in the letter.

205
00:14:21.310 --> 00:14:27.390
<v Speaker 1>Once the inward disposition is set, and only then, the external structure of the society can be safely built.

206
00:14:27.950 --> 00:14:32.350
<v Speaker 1>We move now from the unseen axle in the hub to the visible spokes of the wheel.

207
00:14:33.110 --> 00:14:39.070
<v Speaker 1>The imam walks the governor through the interdependence of the wheel, spoke by spoke, analyzing the diverse classes of society.

208
00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:45.710
<v Speaker 2>But before naming the specific classes, he gives crucial advice on the faculties required to govern them effectively.

209
00:14:46.030 --> 00:14:48.510
<v Speaker 2>He tells the governor to study long with the scholars.

210
00:14:48.590 --> 00:14:50.110
<v Speaker 1>Those who know the formal rules.

211
00:14:50.190 --> 00:14:53.310
<v Speaker 2>Those who know the letter of the law as fixed in the foundational texts.

212
00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:58.910
<v Speaker 2>But he insists the governor must also converse long with the sages, those who know how to apply those rules.

213
00:14:59.190 --> 00:15:01.390
<v Speaker 2>And that requires the living spirit of wisdom.

214
00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:10.350
<v Speaker 2>Knowing the rule and knowing how to lay that rule gently against a messy, breathing, complex human situation, those are two entirely different competences.

215
00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:15.150
<v Speaker 1>A ruler armed only with formal rules devoid of wisdom is incredibly dangerous.

216
00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:21.870
<v Speaker 1>They become a rigid tyrant of bureaucracy, enforcing the letter of the law even when it crushes the spirit of justice.

217
00:15:22.670 --> 00:15:26.110
<v Speaker 1>Wisdom is knowledge put into practice with compassion and timing.

218
00:15:26.430 --> 00:15:32.190
<v Speaker 2>A truly just society requires both the structural integrity of the letter and the flexible humanity of the spirit.

219
00:15:33.070 --> 00:15:41.470
<v Speaker 1>And with that foundation, the interdependence of the classes is framed as a journey from the outermost defense of society inward to its central axis.

220
00:15:41.950 --> 00:15:44.910
<v Speaker 1>The first spoke of the wheel we encounter is the army.

221
00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:51.950
<v Speaker 2>The instruction regarding the military is fascinating because it defies almost every conventional expectation we have about martial leadership.

222
00:15:52.510 --> 00:15:57.790
<v Speaker 2>The Imam instructs that the commander of the troops must be marked above all by a specific quality called him.

223
00:15:57.950 --> 00:16:00.510
<v Speaker 1>This denotes a steady, unhurried forbearance.

224
00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:07.710
<v Speaker 2>He must be gentle with the weak and firm, with the strong, quick to pardon and slow to anger, sincere in his devotion and pure of heart.

225
00:16:07.870 --> 00:16:12.990
<v Speaker 1>Notice how completely strange that portrait is for a soldier, let alone the supreme commander of an army.

226
00:16:13.390 --> 00:16:16.430
<v Speaker 1>Nearly every quality named is a quality of gentleness.

227
00:16:16.670 --> 00:16:22.910
<v Speaker 2>The martial virtues, bravery, tactical brilliance, physical strength, they're almost taken for granted.

228
00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:29.550
<v Speaker 2>What is specified, what is elevated as the deciding factor for command, or the softer virtues?

229
00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:32.830
<v Speaker 2>Mildness, mercy, the readiness to forgive.

230
00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:35.710
<v Speaker 2>But can a soldier really be gentle and fierce?

231
00:16:35.710 --> 00:16:38.550
<v Speaker 2>Doesn't the defense of a state require a hardening of the heart?

232
00:16:39.310 --> 00:16:42.670
<v Speaker 2>We expect martial leaders to be ruthless in the protection of their borders.

233
00:16:42.670 --> 00:16:47.150
<v Speaker 2>We study generals throughout history who were celebrated for their cold, calculated aggression.

234
00:16:47.790 --> 00:16:54.190
<v Speaker 2>Injecting gentleness into the application of lethal force seems contradictory to the very nature of warfare and state defense.

235
00:16:54.670 --> 00:17:00.030
<v Speaker 2>If an army is gentle, doesn't it just get destroyed by an enemy that possesses no such moral reservations?

236
00:17:00.590 --> 00:17:06.350
<v Speaker 1>It seems deeply contradictory until we look at the shared vision between the Quran and the philosophical tradition of Plato.

237
00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:10.990
<v Speaker 1>Plato grappled intensely with the central paradox of the guardian class in his ideal city.

238
00:17:11.070 --> 00:17:12.510
<v Speaker 2>He asked a fundamental question.

239
00:17:12.670 --> 00:17:20.990
<v Speaker 1>How do you raise individuals fierce enough to defend the state from external threats without their fierceness turning inward on the very people they are meant to guard?

240
00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:24.270
<v Speaker 1>If you create a weapon sharp enough to cut your enemies,

241
00:17:24.830 --> 00:17:26.670
<v Speaker 1>How do you keep it from cutting your own hand?

242
00:17:26.870 --> 00:17:28.350
<v Speaker 2>It's A terrifying risk.

243
00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:35.150
<v Speaker 1>Plato concluded they must be fundamentally gentle to their own people and dangerous only to those who threaten the peace.

244
00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:47.070
<v Speaker 1>The Quran, in chapter 48, demands the exact same equilibrium of those who stand with the prophet, describing them as severe against the disbelievers, yet deeply compassionate amongst themselves.

245
00:17:47.230 --> 00:17:48.590
<v Speaker 2>I have to concede that point.

246
00:17:48.670 --> 00:17:52.830
<v Speaker 2>If gentleness isn't the foundation, strength just becomes indiscriminate brutality.

247
00:17:53.070 --> 00:17:57.470
<v Speaker 2>Without that unhurried forbearance, the military force of a state inevitably turns inward.

248
00:17:57.950 --> 00:18:04.950
<v Speaker 2>History is littered with examples of military coup and police states where the very forces built to protect the citizens end up consuming them.

249
00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:11.030
<v Speaker 1>The foundation of gentleness acts as a necessary regulatory mechanism for the violence inherent in defense.

250
00:18:11.830 --> 00:18:14.070
<v Speaker 1>The ideal warrior is not a tempered brute.

251
00:18:14.710 --> 00:18:21.430
<v Speaker 1>They are a person in whom ferocity and tenderness are both fully present, held in perfect tension, and correctly aimed.

252
00:18:21.830 --> 00:18:25.750
<v Speaker 1>Gentleness is the very thing that makes their immense strength safe to unleash.

253
00:18:26.150 --> 00:18:32.310
<v Speaker 2>From the outermost defense of the army, the text moves inward to the next spoke of the wheel, the judges.

254
00:18:32.710 --> 00:18:38.550
<v Speaker 2>If the army guards the physical borders of the state, the judges guard the conceptual borders of truth and justice.

255
00:18:38.870 --> 00:18:43.510
<v Speaker 1>The portrait of the judge drawn here is one of a soul the world simply cannot bend.

256
00:18:44.230 --> 00:18:47.270
<v Speaker 1>The judge faces two primary unceasing temptations.

257
00:18:47.830 --> 00:18:56.550
<v Speaker 1>First, the intellectual complexity of a hard case, which constantly tempts the judge to reach for the nearest easy answer rather than doing the grueling work of uncovering the true one.

258
00:18:56.550 --> 00:19:04.550
<v Speaker 2>And second, the psychological pressure of the litigants, who will try every conceivable lever of provocation, flattery, and appeal to move the judge off the truth.

259
00:19:04.710 --> 00:19:11.110
<v Speaker 1>The judge must be unflinching before the truth, free from greed, unsatisfied with easy answers, and entirely immune to pressure.

260
00:19:11.270 --> 00:19:16.950
<v Speaker 2>What is so refreshing here is the imam's deeply tragmatic observation that follows this lofty ideal.

261
00:19:17.510 --> 00:19:21.270
<v Speaker 2>He notes, quite bluntly, that such people are indeed rare.

262
00:19:21.510 --> 00:19:24.710
<v Speaker 2>He does not harbor any romantic illusions about human nature.

263
00:19:25.030 --> 00:19:32.550
<v Speaker 1>He knows that finding individuals with that level of incorruptible spiritual bedrock is like finding diamonds in the street.

264
00:19:32.830 --> 00:19:36.950
<v Speaker 2>Because such souls are rare, his response is not to just hope for the best.

265
00:19:37.030 --> 00:19:39.430
<v Speaker 2>His response is purely structurally practical.

266
00:19:39.590 --> 00:19:40.790
<v Speaker 2>Pay them generously.

267
00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:43.190
<v Speaker 1>It is such a beautifully pragmatic approach.

268
00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:46.470
<v Speaker 1>The text is not saying, just hire angels to sit on the bench.

269
00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:46.950
<v Speaker 1>It is saying,

270
00:19:47.310 --> 00:20:01.590
<v Speaker 1>Hire the best humans you can find, but pay them so exceptionally well that when a wealthy litigant tries to bribe them with a luxury chariot or a modern equivalent like a lavish vacation or a lucrative consulting contract, the judge can simply say, thanks, but I already have everything I need.

271
00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:06.070
<v Speaker 2>Remove any financial anxiety so their need for help from others is entirely diminished.

272
00:20:06.470 --> 00:20:09.990
<v Speaker 2>A judge who wants for nothing materially owes nothing to anyone politically.

273
00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:14.950
<v Speaker 1>Only such a judge is safe to trust with a verdict that might anger the powerful.

274
00:20:15.670 --> 00:20:23.510
<v Speaker 1>Incorruptibility is recognized as partly a profound matter of internal character, but also crucially, partly a matter of adequate salary.

275
00:20:24.070 --> 00:20:32.390
<v Speaker 2>For the administrators who handle the state's daily affairs, the bureaucrats and the custodians of public funds, the advice is intensely vigilant.

276
00:20:32.470 --> 00:20:34.470
<v Speaker 2>Test their honesty before employing them.

277
00:20:34.790 --> 00:20:37.670
<v Speaker 2>Allow no partiality or nepotism in their appointments.

278
00:20:37.870 --> 00:20:40.230
<v Speaker 2>And once they are in place, keep watching them.

279
00:20:40.550 --> 00:20:45.030
<v Speaker 1>Any abuse of privilege, no matter how small, must be caught and punished firmly.

280
00:20:45.350 --> 00:20:48.950
<v Speaker 1>Trust is extended, yes, but it is extended with the eyes wide open.

281
00:20:49.350 --> 00:20:55.510
<v Speaker 1>It is never allowed to harden into a naive assumption that an honest person stays honest simply because they were honest yesterday.

282
00:20:55.670 --> 00:21:02.710
<v Speaker 2>Proximity to wealth and unmonitored power has a corrosive, corrupting pull on the human soul, and the system must account for that gravity.

283
00:21:02.710 --> 00:21:08.070
<v Speaker 1>Next, we look at the tillers of the soil, the agricultural class that forms the economic base of the society.

284
00:21:08.390 --> 00:21:14.790
<v Speaker 1>The economic advice given here is quietly, profoundly radical, especially when we compare it to how empires usually operate.

285
00:21:15.030 --> 00:21:21.590
<v Speaker 2>The strict instruction is to let the concern with the cultivation of the land outweigh the concern with the collection of the tax.

286
00:21:21.710 --> 00:21:26.390
<v Speaker 1>Let's unpack that because it flies in the face of modern economic short-termism.

287
00:21:27.350 --> 00:21:31.750
<v Speaker 1>Tax without cultivation starves the very ground it draws from.

288
00:21:32.470 --> 00:21:39.910
<v Speaker 1>A ruler fixated on the immediate tax revenue rather than the harvest, the long-term health of the soil and the farmers,

289
00:21:40.470 --> 00:21:42.310
<v Speaker 1>is literally eating their own kingdom.

290
00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:53.270
<v Speaker 2>It is the equivalent of a modern corporation slashing its research and development budget, firing its most experienced staff, and cutting corners on safety, all just to show a spike in quarterly profits.

291
00:21:53.830 --> 00:21:58.070
<v Speaker 2>It looks like success on a spreadsheet today, but it guarantees bankruptcy tomorrow.

292
00:21:58.070 --> 00:22:03.670
<v Speaker 1>The true wealth of the land is the flourishing of the people who work it, not the sum of coins collected from them in a single season.

293
00:22:04.470 --> 00:22:10.550
<v Speaker 1>The humblest cultivator must have as real and direct a claim on the governor's ear as the most powerful general.

294
00:22:10.790 --> 00:22:15.030
<v Speaker 2>The imam diagnoses the deeper spiritual disease behind a ruined land.

295
00:22:15.670 --> 00:22:23.910
<v Speaker 2>Land falls to ruin through the destitution of its people, and that destitution takes root when a governor's soul is given over entirely to amassing wealth.

296
00:22:24.710 --> 00:22:29.270
<v Speaker 2>The text explicitly says this happens when the leaders have doubts about what endures.

297
00:22:29.990 --> 00:22:35.190
<v Speaker 1>Greed is framed here not primarily as an uncontrollable appetite, but as a blindness.

298
00:22:35.590 --> 00:22:43.510
<v Speaker 1>A person reaches so desperately for material wealth only because the enduring reality of the spiritual world has slipped from their sight.

299
00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:52.150
<v Speaker 2>This connects directly to chapter 104 of the Quran, which warns woe to the one who amasses wealth and obsessively counts it, thinking his wealth will render him immortal.

300
00:22:52.230 --> 00:22:55.190
<v Speaker 1>The hoarder counts coins as though they can purchase permanents.

301
00:22:55.350 --> 00:22:59.870
<v Speaker 1>What looks like a single unjust economic policy over taxing the farmers is actually

302
00:22:59.990 --> 00:23:02.710
<v Speaker 1>the tail end of a much longer chain of spiritual decay.

303
00:23:02.870 --> 00:23:05.990
<v Speaker 2>It begins with a soul that has stopped perceiving what actually lasts.

304
00:23:06.390 --> 00:23:08.870
<v Speaker 2>That blindness gives way to the panic of greed.

305
00:23:09.190 --> 00:23:12.150
<v Speaker 2>The greed gives way to the systemic exploitation of the vulnerable.

306
00:23:12.510 --> 00:23:17.670
<v Speaker 2>And the exploitation gives way, finally, to a wasted, exhausted land and a broken society.

307
00:23:17.910 --> 00:23:21.270
<v Speaker 1>Greed is a perceptual failure before it is a moral one.

308
00:23:21.830 --> 00:23:24.950
<v Speaker 1>Oppression is just its outward inescapable fruit.

309
00:23:25.510 --> 00:23:27.750
<v Speaker 2>This brings us to the merchants and the artisans.

310
00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:30.150
<v Speaker 2>The final names spoke before we reach the center.

311
00:23:30.550 --> 00:23:32.790
<v Speaker 2>The attitude toward them is carefully balanced.

312
00:23:32.950 --> 00:23:37.750
<v Speaker 2>They are recognized as the vital veins through which a society's goods and needs travel.

313
00:23:37.990 --> 00:23:41.190
<v Speaker 1>The instruction is that they should be left to pursue their trades undisturbed.

314
00:23:41.590 --> 00:23:43.590
<v Speaker 1>But peace is not the same as neglect.

315
00:23:43.990 --> 00:23:53.670
<v Speaker 1>The market is prone to its own specific vices, hoarding goods to drive up prices, miserliness, and the subtle cheating of customers through false weights or deceitful contracts.

316
00:23:53.910 --> 00:23:55.150
<v Speaker 2>A trade left entirely

317
00:23:55.190 --> 00:23:57.750
<v Speaker 2>unwatched curdles rapidly into exploitation.

318
00:23:57.910 --> 00:24:02.070
<v Speaker 2>The instruction is to leave them free to innovate and trade, but not unwatched by the eye of justice.

319
00:24:02.310 --> 00:24:05.190
<v Speaker 2>The market is a fantastic servant, but a tyrannical master.

320
00:24:05.350 --> 00:24:17.510
<v Speaker 1>What we're seeing across all these diverse classes, the gentle soldiers, the highly paid judges, the scrutinized administrators, the protected tillers, the regulated merchants, is the classical Ismaili reading deeply present in these teachings.

321
00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:19.910
<v Speaker 1>Faith and the world are one seamless fabric.

322
00:24:19.990 --> 00:24:21.910
<v Speaker 2>Governance is an anatomy of worship.

323
00:24:22.070 --> 00:24:25.070
<v Speaker 1>The proper functioning of the state is not some sect

324
00:24:25.150 --> 00:24:29.110
<v Speaker 1>administrative tasks that you do on weekdays, separate from your spiritual duty on the weekend.

325
00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:39.350
<v Speaker 2>The administration of justice, the careful oversight of the market, the fierce protection of the agricultural land, these are not just good policies, they are profound spiritual obligations.

326
00:24:39.830 --> 00:24:43.190
<v Speaker 2>They are how a leader prays with their hands and their decrees.

327
00:24:43.430 --> 00:24:48.870
<v Speaker 1>This is onerous for the governors, but God makes it light for those who aspire to the hereafter.

328
00:24:49.910 --> 00:24:54.550
<v Speaker 1>We arrive now at the passage the imam most wants the governor to take to heart.

329
00:24:56.070 --> 00:24:59.510
<v Speaker 1>Having mapped the spokes of the wheel, we reach the lowest spoke.

330
00:24:59.990 --> 00:25:00.870
<v Speaker 1>We reach the poor.

331
00:25:01.030 --> 00:25:04.470
<v Speaker 2>This is a class with no resources, no leverage, and no voice whatsoever.

332
00:25:04.790 --> 00:25:12.230
<v Speaker 2>We are talking about the destitute, the crippled, the orphans, the elderly without family, and crucially, those who are too proud to beg.

333
00:25:12.390 --> 00:25:17.270
<v Speaker 1>Their need is entirely invisible to the state apparatus precisely because they will not display in the public square.

334
00:25:17.590 --> 00:25:20.950
<v Speaker 2>These are the people most desperately in need of justice from their ruler.

335
00:25:21.270 --> 00:25:25.790
<v Speaker 2>They represent a treatment for which God alone can excuse the ruler on the day they find

336
00:25:25.870 --> 00:25:26.550
<v Speaker 2>finally meet.

337
00:25:27.430 --> 00:25:36.870
<v Speaker 2>The imam instructs the governor to appoint a specific, dedicated officer with the explicit charge of uncovering these hidden needs and attending to them directly.

338
00:25:37.110 --> 00:25:43.030
<v Speaker 1>This is the sharpest test of any just rule, because no ruler can extract a single ounce of profit from them.

339
00:25:43.670 --> 00:25:49.350
<v Speaker 1>The lowest spoke of the wheel is the furthest from the halls of power and the easiest to conveniently forget.

340
00:25:49.510 --> 00:25:55.790
<v Speaker 2>The charge to institutionalize this level of care, to actively go looking for the need that hides itself in the shadows

341
00:25:55.910 --> 00:26:02.630
<v Speaker 2>rather than passively waiting at a polished counter for the broken to appear and fill out a bureaucratic form, is incredibly profound.

342
00:26:02.790 --> 00:26:06.150
<v Speaker 1>It establishes a historical through line that echoes across centuries.

343
00:26:06.470 --> 00:26:15.750
<v Speaker 1>We see this exact instinct, this exact spiritual mandate pressed into the modern language of global institutions by the Aga Khan across his decades of guidance.

344
00:26:15.990 --> 00:26:24.390
<v Speaker 2>A community, a nation, is ultimately measured not by its GDP or its military might, but by how it treats those with the least standing to demand anything at all.

345
00:26:24.550 --> 00:26:25.790
<v Speaker 1>Real care means

346
00:26:25.910 --> 00:26:28.390
<v Speaker 1>mobilizing resources to search for the overlooked.

347
00:26:29.110 --> 00:26:38.950
<v Speaker 1>The officer appointed by the imam to uncover invisible need is the conceptual seed from which that entire modern ethic of proactive, systemic institutional care grows.

348
00:26:39.030 --> 00:26:43.270
<v Speaker 2>We must honestly concede that the worldly burden of this directive is immense.

349
00:26:43.670 --> 00:26:56.870
<v Speaker 2>The demand to dedicate precious state resources to care for those from whom no political favor, no tax revenue, and no economic advantage can ever be won is, by any horizontal worldly calculus, completely crushing.

350
00:26:56.950 --> 00:27:04.550
<v Speaker 1>It costs resources, it drains time, it demands relentless focus, and it returns absolutely nothing that the state's ledger can measure as a profit.

351
00:27:04.710 --> 00:27:09.430
<v Speaker 2>In the cold, horizontal accounting of political survival, the poor are pure cost.

352
00:27:09.910 --> 00:27:14.670
<v Speaker 2>A ruler whose horizon stops at this physical life will inevitably eventually take the easy Rd.

353
00:27:14.670 --> 00:27:19.190
<v Speaker 2>and let the poor fall away simply because the brutal math of worldly power demands it.

354
00:27:19.270 --> 00:27:23.990
<v Speaker 1>But the text counters that horizontal accounting directly with a startling teaching from the prophet.

355
00:27:24.150 --> 00:27:29.670
<v Speaker 1>He stated in the bluntest possible terms that a people is granted its victory and its provision through its weak.

356
00:27:29.670 --> 00:27:30.990
<v Speaker 2>Let that sink in for a moment.

357
00:27:31.190 --> 00:27:39.350
<v Speaker 1>The sustenance of the strong, the very success of the entire society, reaches them by way of the very people the strong are constantly tempted to

358
00:27:39.510 --> 00:27:40.390
<v Speaker 1>look and discard.

359
00:27:40.790 --> 00:27:43.190
<v Speaker 1>The destitute are not dead weight on the wheel of state.

360
00:27:43.590 --> 00:27:50.150
<v Speaker 1>They are the unseen channel, the hidden conduit through which the society's actual life supply and divine favor is delivered.

361
00:27:50.670 --> 00:27:59.750
<v Speaker 2>When this duty of care is charged with real faith in God's ultimate justice and a real burning longing for the hereafter, it ceases to be a burden to be shed.

362
00:28:00.150 --> 00:28:03.990
<v Speaker 2>It becomes an inescapable, joyous consequence of belief.

363
00:28:04.230 --> 00:28:08.070
<v Speaker 1>What makes this heavy load bearable is entirely the spiritual dimension.

364
00:28:08.790 --> 00:28:16.230
<v Speaker 1>It is only when this physical world is seen transparently through to the next that a fully just attitude toward this world becomes practically possible.

365
00:28:16.310 --> 00:28:21.590
<v Speaker 2>The imam lists the qualities that make the load light in a very specific, deliberate psychological order.

366
00:28:21.910 --> 00:28:24.790
<v Speaker 2>First comes aspiration for the hereafter, setting the ultimate goal.

367
00:28:25.030 --> 00:28:27.990
<v Speaker 2>Second is patience, the endurance required for the journey.

368
00:28:28.390 --> 00:28:31.070
<v Speaker 2>Third is trust in the absolute truth of God's promise.

369
00:28:31.110 --> 00:28:33.030
<v Speaker 1>Placing patience second is crucial.

370
00:28:33.590 --> 00:28:36.790
<v Speaker 1>Once a person genuinely sees the absolute values at stake,

371
00:28:37.190 --> 00:28:46.550
<v Speaker 1>Once they perceive the reality of the hereafter, patience is no longer a matter of gritting the teeth, clenching the fists, and enduring the misery by sheer force of will.

372
00:28:46.630 --> 00:28:48.630
<v Speaker 2>It becomes something entirely different.

373
00:28:48.790 --> 00:28:50.310
<v Speaker 2>It is sustained by grace.

374
00:28:50.310 --> 00:28:57.350
<v Speaker 2>It flows naturally from a living faith rather than drawing from a finite, easily exhausted reserve of personal resolve.

375
00:28:57.350 --> 00:29:04.950
<v Speaker 1>A person who has truly realized this vertical connection cannot be talked out of their duty by any appeal to self-interest or political pragmatism.

376
00:29:05.270 --> 00:29:06.870
<v Speaker 1>Their fidelity to the destitute

377
00:29:07.150 --> 00:29:11.750
<v Speaker 1>does not rest on a calculation that a clever, more cynical calculation could overturn.

378
00:29:11.750 --> 00:29:14.950
<v Speaker 2>It rests on something the worldly calculators cannot even see.

379
00:29:15.270 --> 00:29:18.550
<v Speaker 2>To be blind to this reality is the defining mark of the oppressor.

380
00:29:19.190 --> 00:29:23.350
<v Speaker 2>The oppressor violates the compassion that lies at the very heart of the divine nature.

381
00:29:23.750 --> 00:29:29.030
<v Speaker 2>They are blind to the promise of eternal felicity and entirely deaf to the warning of ultimate retribution.

382
00:29:29.190 --> 00:29:32.950
<v Speaker 1>Which brings the letter's deepest, most transformative theme to the surface.

383
00:29:33.670 --> 00:29:35.350
<v Speaker 1>It is the question of pure intention.

384
00:29:36.270 --> 00:29:42.310
<v Speaker 1>What ultimately sustains a human being in a grueling task that yields them nothing visible?

385
00:29:42.310 --> 00:29:44.390
<v Speaker 1>No thanks, no wealth, no fame.

386
00:29:44.870 --> 00:29:48.550
<v Speaker 2>The answer is that they are no longer acting for the visible yield at all.

387
00:29:48.710 --> 00:29:53.590
<v Speaker 1>The imam draws a famous, brilliant, threefold distinction of human worship.

388
00:29:54.070 --> 00:29:59.830
<v Speaker 1>He says there is a group who worship God out of desire for reward, and this is the worship of the merchants.

389
00:30:00.390 --> 00:30:04.630
<v Speaker 1>There is a group who worship out of fear of punishment, and this is the worship of the slaves.

390
00:30:05.310 --> 00:30:09.430
<v Speaker 1>And there is a group who worship out a profound gratitude, and this is the worship of the free.

391
00:30:09.830 --> 00:30:14.150
<v Speaker 2>The merchant serves for a reward not yet in hand, treating faith as a transaction.

392
00:30:14.550 --> 00:30:18.790
<v Speaker 2>The slave serves to avoid a consequence, treating faith as a survival mechanism.

393
00:30:19.270 --> 00:30:23.190
<v Speaker 2>But the free person serves out of overwhelming thankfulness for what they have already been given.

394
00:30:23.270 --> 00:30:26.230
<v Speaker 1>This highest posture is gratitude for what is already possessed.

395
00:30:26.630 --> 00:30:30.150
<v Speaker 1>The logic of gratitude turns on a striking, beautiful inversion of time.

396
00:30:30.630 --> 00:30:33.670
<v Speaker 1>For the one whose certainty of the reward to come is absolute,

397
00:30:34.030 --> 00:30:37.270
<v Speaker 1>The future has, in a spiritual sense, already arrived.

398
00:30:37.430 --> 00:30:40.470
<v Speaker 2>Chapter 108 of the Quran speaks perfectly in this register.

399
00:30:41.110 --> 00:30:45.670
<v Speaker 2>We have given thee the paradisal fountain of abundance, so pray to thy Lord and sacrifice.

400
00:30:46.150 --> 00:30:48.150
<v Speaker 1>The gift is stated in the past tense.

401
00:30:48.310 --> 00:30:49.590
<v Speaker 1>It is already yours.

402
00:30:50.150 --> 00:30:56.630
<v Speaker 1>The worship, the striving for justice, follows as its natural consequence, not as its purchase price.

403
00:30:57.190 --> 00:31:03.590
<v Speaker 1>This is worship as thanksgiving for a treasure already held in the heart, not a desperate petition for a wage owed.

404
00:31:04.310 --> 00:31:11.190
<v Speaker 2>A person who serves out of gratitude for a good already received cannot be discouraged by the worldly failure of their efforts.

405
00:31:11.670 --> 00:31:15.990
<v Speaker 2>The ground of their service was never the visible success of those efforts in the 1st place.

406
00:31:16.070 --> 00:31:28.150
<v Speaker 1>The Quran gives this posture a living, breathing face in chapter 76, describing those who feed the needy, the orphan, and the prisoner, saying they feed them only for the sake of God, desiring neither reward nor thanks.

407
00:31:28.470 --> 00:31:33.990
<v Speaker 2>They do not even desire the incredibly small human return of being thanked by the person eating the bread.

408
00:31:34.550 --> 00:31:38.150
<v Speaker 2>The gift is aimed past every physical recipient, straight at the real.

409
00:31:38.310 --> 00:31:43.430
<v Speaker 1>This introduces the third and final governing analogy of our deep dive, drawn from an early Sufi master.

410
00:31:44.070 --> 00:31:45.990
<v Speaker 1>A just deed is a lifeless form.

411
00:31:46.310 --> 00:31:49.110
<v Speaker 1>Its soul is the secret of sincerity hidden within it.

412
00:31:49.590 --> 00:31:54.630
<v Speaker 1>A form without a soul is merely a corpse, however perfectly correct its outward shape might be.

413
00:31:54.790 --> 00:32:01.750
<v Speaker 2>The soul of any just act, any policy, any decree is the intention that no human onlooker can possibly inspect.

414
00:32:02.230 --> 00:32:09.830
<v Speaker 2>The just person intends justice for its own inherent sake because it reflects the divine, not for any political reward or historical legacy it might fetch.

415
00:32:10.550 --> 00:32:19.190
<v Speaker 2>I don't buy that yet is injustices fulfilled in the intention just an excuse for a ruler who fails, a way to legitimate real injustice by saying, well, I meant well.

416
00:32:19.510 --> 00:32:30.470
<v Speaker 2>If a leader implements policies that cause actual harm or fails to protect the weak because of their own incompetence, can they simply retreat to the inner sanctum of their supposedly pure intention and claim they achieve justice in the unseen realm?

417
00:32:30.870 --> 00:32:34.790
<v Speaker 2>That seems like an incredibly dangerous license for passivity and systemic failure.

418
00:32:35.030 --> 00:32:37.990
<v Speaker 1>The text guards against that dangerous interpretation vehemently.

419
00:32:38.590 --> 00:32:40.150
<v Speaker 1>And it is vital we understand how.

420
00:32:41.030 --> 00:32:48.390
<v Speaker 1>The sincerity of an inner intention is only proven by ceaseless, unyielding, striving toward its realization in the physical world.

421
00:32:48.630 --> 00:32:50.550
<v Speaker 2>So they can't just rest on their thoughts.

422
00:32:50.710 --> 00:32:58.870
<v Speaker 1>Without concordant, exhausting, unyielding action, the intention is exposed as shallow, hypocritical, and merely theoretical.

423
00:32:59.510 --> 00:33:06.310
<v Speaker 1>The truly just person does not rest content in a warm feeling of good intention, while injustice stands tall in the physical world.

424
00:33:06.550 --> 00:33:10.470
<v Speaker 1>Their effort is as tireless as the reward they believe in is inexhaustible.

425
00:33:10.990 --> 00:33:17.590
<v Speaker 1>Any slackening of that physical effort, any surrender to apathy would expose the intention itself as fundamentally insincere.

426
00:33:17.990 --> 00:33:20.950
<v Speaker 2>So the doctrine does not lower the demand for relentless action.

427
00:33:20.950 --> 00:33:27.270
<v Speaker 2>What it does is remove the despair when honest, grueling action is thwarted by the messy reality of worldly politics.

428
00:33:27.430 --> 00:33:32.710
<v Speaker 1>If a leader acts purely for visible results, then a defeated result completely defeats the leader.

429
00:33:32.710 --> 00:33:33.430
<v Speaker 1>They burn out.

430
00:33:33.430 --> 00:33:34.310
<v Speaker 1>They become cynical.

431
00:33:34.870 --> 00:33:48.630
<v Speaker 1>But if they act to conform their soul to the nature of the rial, the outward frustration of their effort, by circumstance, by political enemies, by the sheer unpredictability of human life, it cannot touch the core of what they were actually doing.

432
00:33:48.870 --> 00:33:54.150
<v Speaker 2>They never despair because achieving a utopia in this fractured world was never the entirety of their aim.

433
00:33:54.230 --> 00:33:58.550
<v Speaker 1>The imam's own life is the ultimate historical proof of this exact principle.

434
00:33:59.110 --> 00:34:04.590
<v Speaker 1>His years of rule were a near continuous succession of bitter conflicts, betrayals, and civil strife.

435
00:34:04.950 --> 00:34:09.030
<v Speaker 1>that prevented him outwardly from enacting much of the systemic justice his vision demanded.

436
00:34:09.590 --> 00:34:14.390
<v Speaker 1>Yet none of it produced in him an ounce of dejection, cynicism, or the smallest slackening of effort.

437
00:34:14.870 --> 00:34:16.870
<v Speaker 1>Piety provides the way out of tribulation.

438
00:34:17.030 --> 00:34:24.630
<v Speaker 2>The text states that for the one who has pious saw towards God, God establishes a way out of tribulation and a light out of darkness.

439
00:34:25.110 --> 00:34:27.750
<v Speaker 2>The way out is not necessarily a political victory.

440
00:34:27.830 --> 00:34:31.670
<v Speaker 2>It is the door in the solid wall of every earthly injustice.

441
00:34:32.270 --> 00:34:40.230
<v Speaker 2>It is the internal light that no darkness of external circumstance can finally close over the soul that keeps its face turned relentlessly toward the real.

442
00:34:40.950 --> 00:34:45.190
<v Speaker 2>And this brings us all the way back to the hidden spring we started with, back to the unseen axle.

443
00:34:46.110 --> 00:34:49.190
<v Speaker 2>The ruler's justice doesn't come from the complex machinery of the state.

444
00:34:49.350 --> 00:34:51.030
<v Speaker 2>It flows entirely from prayer.

445
00:34:51.190 --> 00:35:01.030
<v Speaker 2>All the governor's justice, the fierce gentleness of the army, the incorruptibility of the judges, the self-forgetting care for the poor who can give absolutely nothing in return, it all has to be drawn from somewhere.

446
00:35:01.110 --> 00:35:03.750
<v Speaker 1>It cannot be manufactured by sheer human effort alone.

447
00:35:03.830 --> 00:35:10.150
<v Speaker 2>It drains away under the immense crushing strain of governance unless it is continually daily replenished.

448
00:35:10.870 --> 00:35:13.190
<v Speaker 2>The ruler's justice flows from their prayer.

449
00:35:13.790 --> 00:35:19.190
<v Speaker 2>from the quiet unseen remembrance of God that keeps the vertical axis of their soul open and alive.

450
00:35:19.670 --> 00:35:22.230
<v Speaker 2>That is the still point where the ruler meets their Lord.

451
00:35:22.470 --> 00:35:29.350
<v Speaker 2>This inner sanctum, not the bustling administration of policy, is where the whole of their justice is actually born and sustained.

452
00:35:29.910 --> 00:35:34.790
<v Speaker 1>This is onerous for the governors, but God makes it light for those who aspire to the hereafter.

453
00:35:35.030 --> 00:35:44.470
<v Speaker 1>We've looked at the wheel and its invisible absol, the vital necessity of giving each diverse class its due, and the absolute unyielding claim of the poor at the very bottom of the spokes.

454
00:35:44.870 --> 00:35:48.230
<v Speaker 1>But the true weight of this ancient text does not rest safely in the past.

455
00:35:48.470 --> 00:35:50.790
<v Speaker 1>It demands to be carried forward into your life today.

456
00:35:51.230 --> 00:35:57.270
<v Speaker 1>When you strive for fairness in your own sphere of influence, whether managing a team, raising a family, or navigating your community,

457
00:35:57.710 --> 00:36:05.430
<v Speaker 1>Are you acting as the merchant seeking a return, the slave avoiding A consequence, or the free person acting out of sheer radical gratitude for what you've already been given?
